PTForge
=======

PTForge adds a small IDE to Cisco Packet Tracer. You get an editor, a
JavaScript terminal and a step debugger inside the Extensions menu, plus
around 410 global functions that place devices, cable them, push Cisco IOS
configuration and check the result.

The point is speed. A lab that takes twenty minutes of dragging icons becomes
a dozen lines of script, and the same script works again next week.

    addDevice("R1", "2911", 300, 80);
    addDevice("S1", "2960-24TT", 300, 220);
    addDevice("PC1", "PC-PT", 300, 360);
    addLink("R1", "GigabitEthernet0/0", "S1", "GigabitEthernet0/1", "straight");
    addLink("S1", "FastEthernet0/1", "PC1", "FastEthernet0", "straight");
    setInterfaceIp("R1", "GigabitEthernet0/0", "192.168.10.1/24");
    basicSetup("R1", { secret: "class" });
    pingAll();

Everything goes through the official Packet Tracer IPC API, so the file you
end up with is a normal .pkt that opens without PTForge installed.


Where to start
--------------

* Install: https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/guides/installation.html
* First script: https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/guides/getting-started.html
* Function reference: https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/api/index.html
* What does not work: https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/guides/limitations.html
* Bugs and ideas: https://github.com/r4chan842/PTForge/issues


What is in the box
------------------

Topology
    Devices, modules, cables, layout generators, subnet and VLSM planning.

Cisco IOS
    Interfaces, VLANs, trunks, EtherChannel, STP, VTP, port security, DHCP
    snooping. Static routes, OSPF, OSPFv3, EIGRP, RIP, BGP, redistribution.
    ACLs, NAT, SSH, AAA, HSRP.

End devices and servers
    IPv4 and IPv6 on PCs, laptops and servers. DHCP, DNS, HTTP, FTP, email,
    TFTP and syslog.

Wireless
    SSIDs, WPA2, WEP and MAC filtering.

Checking your work
    Reachability matrix, Lab Check reports with scores, audits, snapshots and
    a diff between two states of the same lab.

The editor
    Tabs, IntelliSense from the function catalog, snippets, a problems panel
    and file dialogs. The terminal runs JavaScript in the Packet Tracer engine
    and can also open a device CLI. The debugger has breakpoints, stepping,
    watches, a call stack and logpoints.

Plugins
    .pf files with permissions, a consent prompt and a checksum.


How it runs
-----------

Packet Tracer loads PTForge as a Script Module. Its own JavaScript engine runs
the file, and the PTForge window is a web view that sends code to that engine.
The functions in the engine talk to Packet Tracer through the IPC objects the
API documents: LogicalWorkspace, Device, CiscoDevice, TerminalLine and the
process objects behind servers and switches.

Commands sent to IOS come back with a status, and lines that the device rejects
are handed back to your script instead of disappearing. A wrong device name, a
port that does not exist or a cable that cannot be made throws an error that
says what went wrong, since the API itself only returns false for most of those
calls and gives no reason.


What you need
-------------

* Packet Tracer 8.1 or newer, with the Extensions scripting menu
* Windows, Linux or macOS, wherever Packet Tracer runs
* Node.js 18 or newer, only for the tests and for building a release

Scripts are ES5 because the Packet Tracer engine is ES5. That rules out arrow
functions, let and const, template strings and a few newer built-ins. The editor
flags them as problems instead of letting them fail at run time. Keep that in
mind when you paste code from elsewhere.


How stable is it
----------------

Public function names and arguments only change in major versions, and every
change is written down in the changelog:
https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/project/CHANGELOG.html

Some parts are still marked as unconfirmed. They are built from the Cisco
documentation and run against a mock of the API in the test suite, which proves
that PTForge sends the right calls but cannot prove that your Packet Tracer
build answers the way the documentation says. The list of those spots is in
https://r4chan842.github.io/PTForge/guides/limitations.html. If one of them
behaves differently in your version, open an issue with the version number and
the script.

Back up your .pkt files before running a big script. Most of the API calls have
no undo.


Tests
-----

    npm test        runs 315 tests against a mock of the Packet Tracer API
    npm run check   regenerates the catalog and the release bundle, then tests

The mock lives in tests/helpers and covers devices, ports, links, canvas items,
the file manager, the command log, servers and the simulator. It is the reason
the suite runs in a few seconds without Packet Tracer installed.


Reading the code
----------------

    src/data    plain tables: device types, cable constants, status codes
    src/lib     pure helpers, no ipc access
    src/core    the bridge to Packet Tracer, the runner, the window
    src/api     the public functions you call from a script
    src/ui      the editor, which runs in its own web view

docs/architecture/overview explains how the pieces fit together.


